  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m Logging is a new subsystem — there's no existing logging layer in this repo to extend
  (just scattered [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m/[38;5;153mconsole.error[39m calls), so this gets the full path: questions → approaches → design → spec,
  and no code until you approve.

  What I found in the repo:

  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m + [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — a browser login form. Calls [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username)[39m, [38;5;153mconsole.log("Login result:",
[39m    [38;5;153mresult)[39m, [38;5;153mconsole.error("Validation error:", ...)[39m. The API call to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a stub.
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — a separate Node CommonJS entry point ([38;5;153mgreet[39m), unrelated to the browser app.
    [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m points [38;5;153mmain[39m at it.
  - No test framework, no build step, no dependencies, no logging library.

  Two disjoint programs, and they'd need genuinely different logging (browser logs have to be shipped off the user's
  machine to help you in production; Node logs go to stdout/a file/a collector). So the first thing I need to pin down
  is scope.

  One flag before the questions: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m logs the username on every login attempt, and any logging that ships off-device
  makes credential-adjacent data a real concern. Whatever we build, I'd want redaction rules in the design rather than
  bolted on later.

